### Introduction

A member has to be declared inside a type's own body before anything outside can write to it. Plain `.` access from the outside can never create a new member -- it can only read or write one that already exists:
###
Castaway {
	name := 'unknown'
}

survivor := Castaway()
survivor.name = 'Jack'      # fine -- name already exists
@assert survivor.name == 'Jack', 'writing to an existing member should work'

# survivor.number = 4       -- raises Code::Cannot_Assign_Undeclared_Identifier, number was never declared


### self doesn't get a shortcut either

`self.member := value` inside a method looks like it might self-declare a brand new member -- it doesn't. Every member, even one only ever touched through `self`, has to be declared in the type's own body first, `Self(;)` (the constructor) included:
###
# Raft { Self (; self.planks := 6 ) }   -- raises Code::Cannot_Assign_Undeclared_Identifier: `planks` was never declared

Raft {
	planks,
	Self (;
		self.planks := 6
	)
}

r := Raft()
@assert r.planks == 6, 'self can write to an already-declared member from inside the constructor'


### An instance's shape never grows after it's declared

Since `self` can't self-declare, a type's own body (`name,`, `planks,`, ...) is the *only* place its members ever come from -- reading straight down the body tells you the whole shape, no method (constructor included) can spring a new one into existence later:
###
Raft2 {
	planks,
	Self (;
		self.planks := 6
	)

	add_sail (;
		self.sail := true   # raises Code::Cannot_Assign_Undeclared_Identifier -- `sail` was never declared in the body
	)
}

r2 := Raft2()
# r2.add_sail() would raise Code::Cannot_Assign_Undeclared_Identifier
@assert r2.planks == 6, 'the declared member still works fine'
